## 2. Prompt Engineering vs Fine-Tuning vs RAG

The course frames these as three separate levers, not competitors:

| Approach | Solves | Weakness |
|---|---|---|
| **Prompt engineering** | Behavior rules, restrictions, tone guardrails (e.g. "never reveal salary info, redirect to HR") | Doesn't add new knowledge |
| **Fine-tuning** | Voice, style, consistent tone (e.g. mimicking a CEO's speech pattern) | Expensive/slow to retrain, no citations, knowledge goes stale, more data can *reduce* accuracy |
| **RAG** | Dynamic, frequently-changing factual knowledge | Doesn't change the model's tone/personality by itself |

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Need{What are you fixing?}
    Need -->|Behavior rules, safety, tone constraints| PE[Prompt Engineering]
    Need -->|Voice, style, consistent persona| FT[Fine-Tuning]
    Need -->|Facts that change over time| RAG[RAG]
    PE --> Combine[Real systems combine all three]
    FT --> Combine
    RAG --> Combine
```

**My take:** the transcript's framing is right but slightly clean — in production you almost always end up doing prompt engineering *around* RAG output too (e.g. "if retrieved context is empty, say you don't know"), so the three aren't cleanly separable, they stack.

**Internet POV:** this matches the mainstream 2025–2026 consensus (Anthropic, OpenAI, and most RAG vendors all say the same) — fine-tuning is for *how* a model talks, RAG is for *what it knows right now*. The nuance the transcript skips: newer long-context models sometimes let you skip retrieval entirely for small corpora (just stuff the whole doc set in context), so "RAG vs long-context stuffing" is a real 2026-era tradeoff worth knowing about, especially since context windows have gotten much cheaper.

