## 4. Semantic Search & Embeddings

Semantic search fixes the "exact word" problem by matching on **meaning** instead of literal text.

**How it works:**
1. An embedding model converts text into a vector (list of numbers) representing meaning.
2. Similar meanings → vectors that sit close together in high-dimensional space.
3. Similarity is measured mathematically (dot product / cosine similarity).

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    T1["'dogs are allowed in the office'"] --> E[Embedding Model]
    T2["'pets in the workplace'"] --> E
    T3["'remote work policy'"] --> E
    E --> V1["Vector: [0.55, 0.12, ...]"]
    E --> V2["Vector: [0.58, 0.15, ...]"]
    E --> V3["Vector: [0.02, 0.91, ...]"]
    V1 -.close together.-> V2
    V3 -.far apart.-> V1
```

### Model size example from the course
| Model | Parameters | Size | Use |
|---|---|---|---|
| all-MiniLM-L6-v2 | 22M | ~90MB | Embeddings, runs locally |
| GPT-3.5 | 175B | ~350GB | Generation |
| GPT-4 | 1.8T | ~3.6TB | Generation |

**Local vs API embeddings:** sentence-transformers models run free, offline, on your laptop. OpenAI-style embedding APIs cost per call and need internet, but tend to be higher quality / more up to date.

**Insight — important nuance the transcript doesn't stress:** all-MiniLM-L6-v2 is a *great teaching model* but it's fairly dated and weak by 2026 standards. Current leaderboards (MTEB / Hugging Face) are topped by much larger, higher-accuracy embedding models. If you're building AgriAssist-style retrieval on Malaysian agri/policy documents, MiniLM is fine to prototype with, but for anything domain-specific or non-English-heavy, a stronger multilingual embedding model will noticeably improve retrieval quality — worth benchmarking before you lock in a production model.

