## 6. Vector Databases

**Why not just compare every vector manually?** With 500 docs × 384 dims, that's ~192,000 calculations *per query*. Doesn't scale — like searching a phone book page by page.

**Solution: indexing.** Pre-organize vectors into "neighborhoods" so search only touches relevant clusters.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Q[Query: dog policy] --> Idx[HNSW Index]
    Idx --> N1[Animal Policies Neighborhood]
    Idx -.skip.-> N2[Health Benefits Neighborhood]
    Idx -.skip.-> N3[Remote Work Neighborhood]
    N1 --> R[Return only relevant matches]
```

### Indexing algorithms mentioned
- **HNSW** (Hierarchical Navigable Small World) — graph of nearest neighbors, most widely used default. Fast + accurate.
- **IVF** (Inverted File) — clusters vectors into buckets, search only relevant buckets.
- **LSH** (Locality Sensitive Hashing) — hashes similar vectors into the same bucket.

### Vector DB options
| DB | Best for |
|---|---|
| **Chroma** | Learning, prototyping, free, Python-native |
| **Pinecone** | Managed, production-scale, pay-per-use |
| **Weaviate** | GraphQL API, hybrid search built-in |

**Important practical detail:** Chroma is **in-memory by default** — data disappears when the program stops. Use `PersistentClient` with a storage path for anything real.

**Insight:** the transcript's DB list is a fair snapshot but the space has moved fast — pgvector (Postgres extension) has become a very popular choice specifically *because* teams don't want to run a separate vector DB alongside their existing PostgreSQL instance. Given your AgriAssist stack already runs PostgreSQL/Supabase, pgvector is worth a serious look before adding Chroma as a separate service — one less moving part to operate.

