## 11. Introduction to MCP (Model Context Protocol)

**The problem MCP solves:** LLMs can only generate text/image/audio — they can't *act*. To let an AI agent book a flight, check a database, or call an API, you traditionally had to hand-write custom integration code for every third-party service, each with different endpoints and response formats.

**MCP = a standard so any AI agent can talk to any tool without custom glue code**, the same way USB-C standardized device charging instead of every device needing its own cable.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Agent[AI Agent] -->|standard protocol| MCPClient[MCP Client]
    MCPClient <-->|JSON-RPC| MCPServer[MCP Server]
    MCPServer --> API1[Airline A API]
    MCPServer --> API2[Airline B API]
    MCPServer --> API3[Internal Database]
```

---

## 12. MCP Architecture — Core Components

An MCP **server** exposes three things to clients:

| Component | What it is | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **Tools** | Actions the agent can invoke | `search_flights`, `create_booking` |
| **Resources** | Reference data the agent can read | Airport codes, policies, FAQs |
| **Prompts** | Server-author-defined prompt templates | "You are a travel assistant, always format dates as..." |

An MCP **client** (embedded in tools like Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf) exposes back to the server:

| Component | What it is |
|---|---|
| **Roots** | Which local folders/files the server is allowed to see |
| **Sampling** | Lets the server ask the client's LLM to do something (e.g. summarize), keeping model choice with the client |
| **Elicitation** | Lets the server ask the *end user* directly for input/confirmation |

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph Server["MCP Server exposes"]
        Tools[Tools]
        Res[Resources]
        Prompts[Prompts]
    end
    subgraph Client["MCP Client exposes"]
        Roots[Roots]
        Sampling[Sampling]
        Elicit[Elicitation]
    end
    Server <-->|JSON-RPC 2.0| Client
```

**Communication protocol:** JSON-RPC 2.0 — client sends `{jsonrpc: "2.0", method, params, id}`, server replies `{jsonrpc: "2.0", result, id}` (or `error`). Transport can be **stdio** (local, simplest, fast — used when client and server are both on your machine) or **HTTP** (remote-hosted servers).

